   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT in relation to authorizing the assessor of the town of Brookhaven,\ncounty of Suffolk, to accept an application for a real property tax exemption\nfrom the International Baptist Church",
    "basePrintNo": "S8863",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8863-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Taxation",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8863\n\nSPONSOR: MATTERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act in relation to authorizing the assessor of the town of Brookha-\nven, county of Suffolk, to accept an application for a real property tax\nexemption from the International Baptist Church\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nNotwithstands the exemption application deadline for the International\nBaptist Church (commonly referred to as the \"Connect Church\") in order\nfor this not for profit religious organization to file an application\nwith the Brookhaven town assessor to obtain a retroactive real property\ntax exemption for the congregation's parsonage.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one authorizes the assessor from the town of Brookhaven, Suffolk\nCounty, to accept an application from the International Baptist Church\nfor an exemption from real property taxes pursuant to section 462 of the\nReal Property Tax law for the 2021-2022 assessment roll. The church\nacquired a parsonage for its clergy on June 16, 2020. The bill author-\nizes the Brookhaven Town Board to provide for any corrections to the\nsubject assessment rolls, canceling any outstanding liens, fines or\nfees, and/or providing any refund of taxes paid on such parcel.\n\nSection two provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe International Baptist Church acquired a parcel of real property\nafter the deadline passed to be able to file for a real property tax\nexemption on their newly acquired parsonage. Section 462 authorizes an\nexemption for the residential dwellings set aside for clergy. Due to the\ntiming of the purchase, they could not apply for an exemption for the\n2021-2022 tax year.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediate.\n",
